## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates understanding of windowed stream processing, stateful aggregations with TTL management, and idempotent output semantics—all critical concepts for reliable event-driven systems.

## Key Concepts
- **Windowing**: Groups time-series events into fixed intervals (tumbling windows) for aggregate computation
- **Per-user state with TTL**: Maintains isolated metrics per user, automatically expiring stale entries to bound memory usage
- **Idempotent outputs**: Ensures deterministic results so duplicate processing (from restarts or retries) produces the same output

## Code Example
```javascript
class WindowedCTR {
  constructor(windowSizeMs = 5000, ttlMs = 60000) {
    this.windowSizeMs = windowSizeMs;
    this.ttlMs = ttlMs;
    this.state = new Map();
  }

  processMessage(msg) {
    const { userId, type, timestamp } = JSON.parse(msg.value);
    const windowStart = Math.floor(timestamp / this.windowSizeMs) * this.windowSizeMs;
    const key = `${userId}:${windowStart}`;

    if (!this.state.has(key)) {
      this.state.set(key, { clicks: 0, impressions: 0, emitted: false, lastUpdate: Date.now() });
    }
    const window = this.state.get(key);
    if (type === 'click') window.clicks++;
    if (type === 'impression') window.impressions++;
    window.lastUpdate = Date.now();

    if (!window.emitted && Date.now() - windowStart >= this.windowSizeMs) {
      this.emitCTR(key, window);
      window.emitted = true;
    }
  }

  commitOffsets(offsets) { /* persist consumer offsets */ }
  windowState() { return Array.from(this.state.entries()); }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle late arrivals or out-of-order messages?
- How would you test at scale?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests cross-region ordering, coordination, failover recovery, and durable state management in a realistic KCNA-like system.

## Key Concepts
- Per-tenant global sequence
- Cross-region coordination
- Durable storage and idempotent processing
- Reordering buffers for in-order emission
- Failure/recovery paths and testing

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal sketch
class Coordinator {
  constructor(store) { this.store = store; }
  allocate(tenantId) {
    const last = this.store[tenantId]?.lastSeq || 0;
    const next = last + 1;
    this.store[tenantId] = { lastSeq: next };
    return next;
  }
  commit(tenantId, seq) {
    // durable commit placeholder
    this.store[tenantId].lastSeq = Math.max(this.store[tenantId].lastSeq, seq);
  }
}
class Consumer {
  constructor(coord) { this.coord = coord; this.buff = new Map(); }
  onMessage(msg) {
    const { tenantId, globalSeq } = msg.meta;
    if (!this.buff.has(tenantId)) this.buff.set(tenantId, new Map());
    const map = this.buff.get(tenantId);
    map.set(globalSeq, msg);
    this.flush(tenantId);
  }
  flush(tenantId) {
    const map = this.buff.get(tenantId);
    let seq = this.coord.peekNext(tenantId);
    while (map.has(seq)) {
      const m = map.get(seq);
      // process(m)
      map.delete(seq);
      seq++;
      this.coord.commit(tenantId, seq);
    }
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle clock skew and coordinator failover?
- What metrics would you surface for latency and duplicates?
